\ ff-test: lines
\ Word names match with ASCII letters folded to lower case and every
\ other byte exactly - the way they are hashed.
\
\ They were compared as UTF-8, case-insensitively, which read past the
\ end of a name ending in a cut-off UTF-8 sequence - defining one twice
\ read out of bounds - and matched non-ASCII letters of another case only
\ when both spellings happened to hash alike.

: p 32 emit . ;

: Twice 2 * ;
3 TWICE p 4 twice p cr

\ \xDA starts a two-byte sequence, cut off by the end of the name.
"variable ab\xDA" evaluate p
"variable ab\xDA" evaluate p
"5 ab\xDA ! ab\xDA @" evaluate p p cr
"ab\xDA\xDA" evaluate p cr

\ Non-ASCII letters are not folded: é and É are two names.
"1 constant é" evaluate p
"é" evaluate p p
"É" evaluate p cr
